"Tracks weighted cohorts across repeated categorical observations while preserving complete cohort paths.\n\nUse when: Linked records span categorical dimensions, or a complete joint frequency table describes their category paths. Joint trajectories matter beyond separate pairwise transition totals.\n\nAvoid when: Only marginal or pairwise totals exist, without linked records or complete joint counts.\n\nVisual grammar: Vertical strata encode category totals at each dimension or wave. Ribbon thickness encodes cohort weight; a continuous ribbon identifies its full sequence of categories.\n\nBuild: Use linked records or complete joint counts; declare missing categories and attrition where temporal. Aggregate identical full trajectories with their counts or weights. Lay out strata in stable orders and draw path-preserving ribbons with labels outside bands.\n\nCheck: Check wave totals reconcile with the cohort definition and attrition. Verify ribbons preserve full paths instead of mixing cohorts at intermediate strata. Confirm consistent weight units and category definitions across waves.\n\nPitfalls: Crossings are often intrinsic to real cohort transitions; never reorder observed categories within a path. Prefer a transition matrix or separate marginal charts when ribbon tracing becomes unreliable.\n\nExample: Follow synthetic learners through enrollment, first assessment, and final outcome while preserving each full categorical trajectory.\n\nAlternatives: Sankey diagram, Chord diagram, Mosaic plot."
